  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1238 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nLatest mid-range model guidance shows continued consistency in \nthe upper ridge shifting and degrading eastward towards the middle\nof the week. Model signals still point towards the upper level \nhigh pressure center to eventually setup across the southeast \nCONUS by the end of the week. The question remains of how long \nthe ridge will remain over the east and how high will heights rise\nnext weekend. For now, Tuesday and Wednesday will continue with \nhot, dry, and windy headlines. PoPs increase considerably Thursday\nonward for much of the CWA, and the NBM has been persistent in \nkeeping these rain chances through the rest of the week. That \nsaid, these 30-60% chances are not evenly spread out across the \nregion. Ensemble guidance continues to favor the northwest \ncombined Panhandles where the potential for better forcing exists.\nBy Friday and into the weekend, high temperatures are still \nforecast to drop back to seasonal values. However, a few 100s \ncannot be ruled out in the southeast Texas Panhandle or Palo Duro \nCanyon. This cooler outcome will also depend on how strong \ntroughing will become over the western US. Long range models are \nstill largely inconsistent in how far south they allow frontal \nboundaries to move into the combined Panhandles. Of course, \nstronger fronts will allow for cooler temperatures; but if these \nfronts stall over parts of the area and retreat back north, The \nsouthern Texas Panhandle may still be on the warmer side and \ntriple digit heat could persist in those areas while the rest of \nthe FA falls back into the 90s.\n\nRangel",
